# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# macOS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Finder writes .DS_Store into every directory it displays, recording view
# settings (icon positions, window size, sort order). Harmless locally, but it
# also embeds the directory listing — including names of since-deleted files —
# which is why it is treated as an information disclosure finding when it ends
# up on a web server.
.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Windows
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Thumbs.db
Thumbs.db:encryptable
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Linux
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*~
.fuse_hidden*
.nfs*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editors
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*.bak
*.tmp

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scratch
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing the audit produces goes here — runs are written to audit-state/runs/
# and are committed. This is for whatever you keep beside the work.
/scratch/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Go
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only if someone builds the script rather than `go run`-ing it.
/compliance-report
/audit-run

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build artefacts — anchored to the root, so nothing inside audit-state/ can
# match them by accident.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/audit-run.log
/*.cis.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Terraform
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# State records the audit identity and every binding it created. It is the
# teardown record and must not be committed.
#
# .terraform/ holds downloaded provider binaries — the google provider alone is
# ~116 MB, over GitHub's 100 MB per-file limit. Never commit it.
#
# terraform.tfvars IS committed: it holds an organization ID, a project ID and
# a list of auditor emails. None of those are credentials, and committing it
# means anyone can reproduce exactly what was applied.
**/.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfplan
tfplan
crash.log

# .terraform.lock.hcl SHOULD be committed — it pins provider versions so every
# operator resolves the same ones. It is small and contains no secrets.
!**/.terraform.lock.hcl

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials — NEVER delete these, in any repository
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The audit runs by impersonation precisely so that no service account key
# exists. Committing one would break safeguard 5.2, which this kit measures.
*.json.key
*-key.json
credentials.json
service-account*.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Results and settings ARE committed
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This repository assumes it is private. audit-state/ is the deliverable, and
# config/audit.env and config/projects.txt are committed with it so that every
# auditor on the engagement scores against identical criteria rather than
# retyping the policy answers. Neither holds a credential.
#
# What that means in practice: repository access is access to the findings.
# Results name every public bucket, every over-permissioned account and every
# open firewall rule in the estate. Keep the access list matched to who is
# allowed to see them, and keep the repository private — if it is ever made
# public, forked, or moved between organizations, the results go with it.
# History outlives the setting.
#
# Add your own paths when you commit. Never `git add -A`.
